=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: dns_server
diagnosis_text:
**Affected component:** The DNS daemon on `dns_server` (10.200.0.2) — expected to run `named`, `bind9`, or `dnsmasq` — is completely absent. Nothing is listening on UDP or TCP port 53.

**Mechanism:** Both user-facing hosts (`host_1_1_1_1` and `host_2_1_1_1`) are configured with `10.200.0.2` as their only nameserver. When they attempt to resolve `web0.local`, `web1.local`, or any other `*.local` hostname, the DNS query goes unanswered — there is no listener on port 53. `nslookup`/`dig` return no addresses, so `curl` immediately exits with code `000` ("couldn't resolve host") before ever attempting a TCP connection. This makes every web portal appear broken even though the web servers, the IP fabric, and OSPF routing are all fully operational.

**Localization:** `dns_server` only. `pgrep` finds no DNS daemon process; `ss` shows no port-53 socket; nftables and iptables carry only Docker-loopback NAT rules (harmless baseline). OSPF adjacencies, all four web servers (HTTP 200 on localhost), and host IP addressing are clean.

**Root cause:** The DNS service on `dns_server` is not running — the daemon was never started or was stopped/killed, leaving port 53 completely unserved.

**Fix:** Start the DNS daemon on `dns_server` (e.g., `service bind9 start` or `named -u bind`). Once the daemon is up and the zone records for `web0.local`–`web3.local` are served, name resolution will succeed and all web portals will immediately become reachable without touching any web server.
=== END ===